WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.927
Telemetry is security-relevant data generated by systems, applications, identities, networks,

00:00:03.927 --> 00:00:05.458
endpoints, and cloud services.

00:00:05.458 --> 00:00:09.668
It can include audit logs, authentication logs, endpoint events, network logs,

00:00:09.668 --> 00:00:13.495
cloud activity records, vulnerability scan results, configuration snapshots, change records,

00:00:13.495 --> 00:00:15.792
tickets, incident records, and compliance evidence.

00:00:15.792 --> 00:00:18.471
Telemetry is the raw material of monitoring,

00:00:18.471 --> 00:00:22.681
but raw data only becomes useful when it is reliable, understandable,

00:00:22.681 --> 00:00:24.212
and available when needed.

00:00:24.212 --> 00:00:25.360
Source reliability matters.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:31.101
A log source that silently stops sending data can create a false sense of visibility.

00:00:31.101 --> 00:00:34.928
A scanner that misses important assets can distort vulnerability priorities.

00:00:34.928 --> 00:00:38.373
A ticket queue with inconsistent fields can weaken reporting.

00:00:38.373 --> 00:00:43.731
A configuration snapshot without timestamps or ownership may be hard to use as evidence.

00:00:43.731 --> 00:00:48.324
Monitoring programs should track the health and quality of their evidence sources,

00:00:48.324 --> 00:00:50.620
not only the events inside them.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:54.447
Retention and time synchronization are practical details with large consequences.

00:00:54.447 --> 00:00:59.040
Retention affects how far back investigators, auditors, and operators can reconstruct activity.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.250
Time synchronization affects whether events from different systems can be correlated.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:06.312
If authentication logs, cloud activity logs, endpoint events,

00:01:06.312 --> 00:01:10.139
and tickets all use inconsistent clocks or short retention windows,

00:01:10.139 --> 00:01:15.497
the organization may struggle to understand what happened during an incident or audit review.

00:01:15.497 --> 00:01:18.176
Evidence should be current, traceable, and reviewable.

00:01:18.176 --> 00:01:22.386
Current means it reflects the system as it actually operates now.

00:01:22.386 --> 00:01:27.745
Traceable means a reviewer can connect evidence back to the asset, control, owner, event,

00:01:27.745 --> 00:01:29.275
or decision it supports.

00:01:29.275 --> 00:01:34.251
Reviewable means it is understandable to the people who need to use it.

00:01:34.251 --> 00:01:39.609
Continuous monitoring improves when evidence quality is treated as part of the security program,

00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:41.140
not as an afterthought.
